import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---
<Layout
  title="How to Build a Relocation Budget — Relocation Cost"
  description="A four-bucket method for planning a relocation budget: moving costs, travel and housing, deposits and setup, and a buffer. Includes how to use the calculator."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 py-14">
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-sky-600">Guide</p>
    <h1 class="mt-2 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight">How to build a relocation budget</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-8 text-slate-600">A useful relocation budget separates the move itself from the costs of getting established at your destination. Many people price the truck or the movers and forget everything else. This four-step method keeps the whole picture in view.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold">Step 1: Create four buckets</h2>
    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 leading-7 text-slate-700">
      <li><strong>Transport and moving services:</strong> truck rental or movers, fuel, tolls, packing materials and any coverage you choose to buy.</li>
      <li><strong>Travel and temporary accommodation:</strong> getting yourself and your household there, plus lodging if housing is not ready on arrival.</li>
      <li><strong>Deposits, setup and essential purchases:</strong> rental deposits, first payments, utility setup fees and items you will not bring with you.</li>
      <li><strong>Contingency:</strong> money set aside for costs you cannot predict yet.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold">Step 2: Put a number on each line</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-slate-700">Start with what you already know, such as a rental deposit or a booked ticket. Request written quotes for the move itself. For everything else, use conservative estimates and write them as ranges while you are unsure. A range is more honest than a single guess, and it is easier to update.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold">Step 3: Add a buffer</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-slate-700">Surprises are normal: a delay in getting housing, a replacement item, an extra day of truck rental. Our calculator adds a 10% buffer to the move and the known extra costs as a planning default. Raise it if your move is complex or your dates are uncertain, and lower it only when most of your costs are already confirmed in writing.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold">Step 4: Replace estimates as quotes arrive</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-slate-700">Update each line when you receive a real price. Keep the date of every quote and note what is included, so you can compare providers fairly and spot what is missing. The goal is a decision-ready budget, not false precision.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold">Using the calculator for a first draft</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-slate-700">The <a class="font-semibold text-sky-700 underline" href="/calculator">calculator</a> gives you a planning estimate for the move from the states, distance, home size and move type you choose. The fields under “Other relocation costs” match the buckets above: travel, temporary housing, deposits and setup, and packing or extras. Enter the amounts you already know and leave the rest at zero until you have a figure.</p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-slate-700">To see how a specific route compares across move types, open a route guide such as <a class="font-semibold text-sky-700 underline" href="/moving-cost/ca-to-tx">California to Texas</a>, or browse <a class="font-semibold text-sky-700 underline" href="/moving-cost">all route guides</a>. To understand how the estimate is built, read the <a class="font-semibold text-sky-700 underline" href="/methodology">methodology</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Layout>
